    "passage": "Rich, flaky and the perfect complement to your morning cup of coffee, the croissant is the classic French pastry. But how did the croissant come to be? Across various accounts of croissant history, most sources agree that it [[1]] in Austria as the kipferl. The kipferl, shaped like a crescent or half moon, was a type of bread roll, often either plain or filled with nuts or other ingredients. Its [[2]] shape is believed to have inspired the modern croissant.\nThe kipferl made its way to France in the 19th century, thanks to Austrian entrepreneur August Zang. In 1839, Zang opened the “Boulangerie Viennoise” in Paris, and [[3]] Viennese baked goods to the French people. The bakery’s success spurred local bakers to experiment with these foreign pastries, leading to the adaptation of the kipferl into what became [[4]] the croissant.\nToday, the croissant is enjoyed in various forms worldwide, inspiring trends in fashion and design. [[5]], croissant-shaped handbags have gained popularity, reflecting the pastry’s cultural significance. The croissant’s journey from the Austrian kipferl to a symbol of French cuisine illustrates the power of cultural exchange in shaping our favorite foods.",
